• maxime

I found the answer, here it is:

The maximum inrush current is not specified as is. But dv/dt is provided and we can calculate the peak current capability using following formula: I(peak)=C*dv/dt. Per example, KEMET Part Number: R46KN422000P0M is a 2.2uF with a 200v/us, it gives a maximum specified peak current of 440A. In our design, we can find the maximum peak current at power up by considering the capacitor shorted, it gives 230VAC / 0.707 / 100 = 3.3A. The maximum peak current in our circuit is less than the maximum current specified by the capacitor, it will work without problem.

Another thing to consider is the power surge in the series resistor at power up. In our case, the surge power will be Ppeak=Vmax*Vmax/Rseries. In the above example, it gives Ppeak=(230VAC/0.707)* (230VAC/0.707)/100=1058W. I simulated the pulse width, I think it’s safe to consider a pulse duration of 1*TAU=1*R*C. In the above example, it gives a pulse duration of 100*2.2uF=220us. You have to make sure the series resistor is able to support 1058W for 220us, SR2512JK-07100RL would be a good choice.

