6061 has 8 divisors (see below), whose sum is σ = 7200. Its totient is φ = 5040.

The previous prime is 6053. The next prime is 6067. The reversal of 6061 is 1606.

6061 = 82 + 92 + ... + 262.

It is a sphenic number, since it is the product of 3 distinct primes.

It is a cyclic number.

It is not a de Polignac number, because 6061 - 23 = 6053 is a prime.

It is a Duffinian number.

6061 is a lucky number.

It is a plaindrome in base 7, base 14 and base 16.

It is a congruent number.

It is not an unprimeable number, because it can be changed into a prime (6067) by changing a digit.

It is a polite number, since it can be written in 7 ways as a sum of consecutive naturals, for example, 195 + ... + 223.

It is an arithmetic number, because the mean of its divisors is an integer number (900).

26061 is an apocalyptic number.

It is an amenable number.

6061 is a deficient number, since it is larger than the sum of its proper divisors (1139).

6061 is a wasteful number, since it uses less digits than its factorization.

6061 is an odious number, because the sum of its binary digits is odd.

The sum of its prime factors is 59.

The product of its (nonzero) digits is 36, while the sum is 13.

The square root of 6061 is about 77.8524244966. The cubic root of 6061 is about 18.2325786085.

Adding to 6061 its reverse (1606), we get a palindrome (7667).

It can be divided in two parts, 60 and 61, that added together give a palindrome (121).

The spelling of 6061 in words is "six thousand, sixty-one".

Divisors: 1 11 19 29 209 319 551 6061

### Challenge Problem Showdown – April 30th, 2012

We invite you to test your GMAT knowledge for a chance to win! Each week, we will post a new Challenge Problem for you to attempt. If you submit the correct answer, you will be entered into that week’s drawing for a free Manhattan GMAT Prep item. Tell your friends to get out their scrap paper and start solving!

Here is this week’s problem:

From 2008 to 2009, the number of Easter eggs in a certain egg hunt rose 20%, then fell 17% from 2009 to 2010. From 2008 to 2009, the ratio of Easter eggs to Easter bunnies in the same hunt fell 20%, then rose 22% from 2009 to 2010. By what approximate percent did the number of bunnies change over the full period from 2008 to 2010?

Discuss this week’s problem with like-minded GMAT takers on our Facebook page.

The weekly winner, drawn from among all the correct submissions, will receive One Year of Access to our Challenge Problem Archive, AND the GMAT Navigator, AND Our Six Computer Adaptive Tests (\$82 value).

1. Naveen kumar Gupta May 7, 2012 at 4:46 am

In 2008
Egg/Bunnies = x/y1= z

In 2009
Egg/Bunnies= 1.2x/y2 = 0.83z

Now change in bunnies will be given by
y2/y1 = 120/83

or in other words % increase in bunnies will be 50% from 2008 to 2009

Now in 2009
x/y1=z

in 2010
0.83x/y2=1.22z

Now change in bunnies will be given by
y2/y1 = 83/122

or in other words % decrease in bunnies will be 33% from 2009 to 2010

So if we assume that initially we have 100 bunnies, then first we have to increase it by 50% & then decrease by 33%

100 ——– 150 —— 103

Which leaves us a reply of 3% increase from 2008 to 2010

# In a triangle

Question:

In a triangle $\mathrm{PQR}$, the co-ordinates of the points $\mathrm{P}$ and $\mathrm{Q}$ are $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-2)$ respectively. If the equation of the perpendicular bisector of $\mathrm{PR}$ is $2 x-y+2=0$, then the centre of the circumcircle of the $\triangle \mathrm{PQR}$ is :

1. (1) $(-1,0)$

2. (2) $(-2,-2)$

3. (3) $(0.2)$

4. (4) $(1,4)$

Correct Option: , 2

Solution:

Equation of perpendicular bisector of $P R$ is

$y=x$

Solving with $2 \mathrm{x}-\mathrm{y}+2=0$ will give

$(-2,2)$

