11221141 has 2 divisors, whose sum is σ = 11221142. Its totient is φ = 11221140.

The previous prime is 11221127. The next prime is 11221159. The reversal of 11221141 is 14112211.

Adding to 11221141 its reverse (14112211), we get a palindrome (25333352).

11221141 is digitally balanced in base 2, because in such base it contains all the possibile digits an equal number of times.

It is a weak prime.

It can be written as a sum of positive squares in only one way, i.e., 10923025 + 298116 = 3305^2 + 546^2 .

It is a cyclic number.

It is a de Polignac number, because none of the positive numbers 2k-11221141 is a prime.

It is a congruent number.

It is not a weakly prime, because it can be changed into another prime (11221181) by changing a digit.

It is a polite number, since it can be written as a sum of consecutive naturals, namely, 5610570 + 5610571.

It is an arithmetic number, because the mean of its divisors is an integer number (5610571).

Almost surely, 211221141 is an apocalyptic number.

It is an amenable number.

11221141 is a deficient number, since it is larger than the sum of its proper divisors (1).

11221141 is an equidigital number, since it uses as much as digits as its factorization.

11221141 is an evil number, because the sum of its binary digits is even.

The product of its digits is 16, while the sum is 13.

The square root of 11221141 is about 3349.7971580381. The cubic root of 11221141 is about 223.8784749513.

The spelling of 11221141 in words is "eleven million, two hundred twenty-one thousand, one hundred forty-one".

# Life Hack: How to Become a Walking Calendar?

### Making Mathematics Great Again

Can you tell the day for any date without a calendar at hand? Is that really possible?

It is actually a simple skill that any one can learn. It is also very practical as you may always consider your availability for an activity or an event or you just need to know the day of anyone’s birthday. All you need is a little bit of practice, then you can quickly and easily tell the day of the week of practically any date in history or in the future.

### Here’s the Secret

You may need to memorize some codes to learn this trick, but they are very easy to remember.

First, we assign a code number to every day of the week.

• Monday – 1
• Tuesday – 2
• Wednesday – 3
• Thursday – 4
• Friday – 5
• Saturday – 6
• Sunday – 7 or 0

Second, we assign a code number for every month of the year. These month codes are used for every year with two exceptions. In a leap year, the month code for January is 5 and for February is 1. The month codes with the corresponding mnemonics are as follows:

• January – 6 (WINTER has 6 letters)
• February – 2 (2nd month)
• March – 2 (You march with 2 feet)
• April – 5 (APRIL has 5 letters)
• May – 0 (MAY0 for mayonnaise)
• June – 3 (JUN has 3 letters)
• July – 5 (JULIE has 5 letters)
• August – 1 (August begins with an A, the 1st letter)
• September – 4 (SEPT has 4 letters)
• October – 6 (Halloween TRICKS or TREATS have 6 letters each)
• November – 2 (2nd last month)
• December – 4 (XMAS has 4 letters)

Third, we assign a code number for every year. For example, the year code for 2011 is 6.

### The Formula

• Day of the week = (Month code + Date + Year Code) mod 7
• Note: mod 7 indicates the remainder you get when you divide by 7.

Examples

What is the day for July 16, 2011?

Day of the week = (Month code + Date + Year Code) mod 7

Day of the week = (5 + 16 + 6) mod 7 = 27 mod 7 = 6 (Therefore, it’s a Saturday)

What is the day for December 25, 2011?

Day of the week = (Month code + Date + Year Code) mod 7

Day of the week = (4 + 25 + 6) mod 7 = 35 mod 7 = 0 (Therefore, it’s a Sunday)

Hooray! With practice, you are now ready to be the walking calendar. Amaze your family, colleagues, students, teachers and everyone else.

