## Frequently Asked Questions

### How do we differentiate between divisor and dividend?

A dividend is a number we divide, while a divisor is a number by which we divide. Divisor comes on second, followed by the dividend that we write first.

For instance, if you have 12 candies and want to distribute them among 3 children, the equation will be 12 ÷ 3. You will put 12 first because this is the number being divided. So here, 12 is a dividend. On the other hand, 3 is written after 12, and it is the number with which we are dividing 12. Hence, 3 is a divisor.

### Which formula is used to find a divisor?

There are two formulas used to find a divisor.

The first one is: Divisor = Dividend ÷ Quotient. This formula is used to find a divisor when the remainder is 0.

Second is: Divisor = (Dividend – Remainder) /Quotient. This formula is used when the remainder is not 0.

### Is there a possibility of a number having the same divisor?

Yes, there is. Every number can be divided by itself, leaving 1 as the quotient. So, it would not be wrong to say that all the numbers can have the same divisors.

Let’s take the example of 5. If we divide 5 by 5 (5 ÷ 5), then 5 will be the divisor of 5. And ultimately, 1 will be the quotient.

### What is the difference between a divisor and a factor?

A divisor is a number with which we can divide any number. However, a factor is different from a divisor. It is the number that can be divided with another number leaving no remainder. All factors are divisors, but not all divisors are factors.

### Is it possible to do division by repeated subtraction?

Fortunately yes. You can do division by repeated subtraction. In repeated subtraction, we continuously subtract a number from a bigger number. It continues until we get the 0 or any other number less than the actual number as a remainder.

However, it can be a lengthy process, so we can use division as a shortcut.

### Can I check the remainder and the quotient in a division problem?

Yes, you can quickly check the remainder and quotient in a division problem by using this relationship:

Dividend = Divisor x Quotient + Remainder

## Find out how to calculate sq. footage?

Fairly straightforward math: Get out your trusty measuring tape to discover a room’s size and width. Then, multiply these two numbers.

But when the room is not a pleasant, neat sq. or rectangle, and it is obtained some outcroppings and jogs, you may should get artistic. Swallow your horror, understand you may by no means have to return there and channel your inside ninth grader: Divide the room into squares or rectangles. Add all the ins and outs collectively to get the scale of your entire area.

Or do the maths on a calculator like this one at Calculator Soup, in which you’ll put within the room’s form, even when it would not have corners. It can also calculate worth per sq. foot in case you want that. And, as a result of it’s 2021, yow will discover a laser measure app in your smartphone.

When you’re house looking and simply want a ballpark, use your stride. The common stride is about 5 ft, however that depends upon so many issues together with peak, age, terrain. Or you may also search for your shoe size and what number of inches that’s; e.g., a girls’s 8 is about 9 ft 5 inches, a males’s 10 is about 10 ft 6 inches. Hold that quantity in your again pocket while you’re out and about.

## Common Residence Dimension and Sq. Footage Developments

As an fascinating aspect observe, homes and residences have modified in sq. footage over time. Within the US, the typical dimension of a single-family house is about 2,600 sq. ft. In 1970, the typical home dimension was 1,660 sq. ft. In 1950, the typical dwelling was 983 sq. ft. (An fascinating aspect observe to the fascinating aspect observe, whereas properties are getting bigger, household dimension is trending smaller. In 1940, there was a mean of three.6 folks per family, according to US Census data. At this time, it’s about 2.5.)

Conversely, at this time’s residences common about 1,015 sq. ft, which is barely smaller than the 1,117 sq. ft in 2011. This could possibly be as a result of extra studios and one-bedroom apartments are being constructed and fewer two and three-bedroom items, in accordance with CoStar information.

For some, smaller is healthier. There’s much less maintenance, cheaper utility payments, and probably much less muddle. Maybe this explains the latest development of tiny homes and micro residences, which is in direct distinction to the bigger home development. A tiny home is 400 sq. ft or much less, whereas a micro house is normally between 100 and 300 sq. ft.

So … how a lot sq. footage do you want? Relying on who resides with you, the quantity of stuff you may have, the scale of your furnishings, and your private desire, wherever between 100 and a pair of,600 sq. ft … kind of.

Not useful? Okay, you’ll in all probability need to slim that down a bit of. When attempting to determine how a lot area you’ll have to be comfy in your house, listed here are some components to contemplate:

## Is There Extra Than One Technique to Measure Sq. Footage?

Seems, there are not less than 5. We have been a bit of shocked as effectively. Right here’s a simplified breakdown:

• Partitions-out technique: measured from the within and brought from architectural drawings.
• ANSI technique: measured from the outside of the property, partitions aren’t subtracted.
• Partitions-in technique Whole Area: contains closets, storage, and non-livable areas.
• Partitions-in technique Livable Area: excludes closets and non-livable areas.
• The estimating technique: sure, it is a factor.

Measuring sq. ft is obscure as a result of nobody is required to outline which technique they used after they connected their sq. footage quantity to the house itemizing. Wouldn’t it’s good if each house adhered to the identical technique?

## Measure every room

Going room by room, measure the size and width, rounding off to the closest half-foot.

Actual property brokers usually use an digital laser distance measuring device. In case you have one, place it on a wall, aiming it instantly on the wall reverse it. You’ll then see the sq. footage displayed on the gadget’s display screen. A tape measure works effectively if you do not have a laser device.

Multiply these numbers, rounding off to the closest sq. foot, then write down your measurement in your sketch. As an example, if the kitchen is 10 ft by 16 ft, the whole sq. footage is 160 sq. ft.

If a room has an alcove, equivalent to a lounge with an space for a home office, measure that area individually and add it to the general sq. footage of the room. The identical is true for rooms with closets: measure each by multiplying the size by width.

## Accuracy of a floorplan

The Strata Plan is the authorized doc registered on the Land Title Workplace that stipulates the true sq. footage of a condominium. Itemizing brokers typically rent professionals to measure and to create a floorplan and gross sq. footage for his or her advertising materials. Take observe that skilled measurers calculate sq. footages which can be usually bigger than the authorized strata plan. I’ve seen a professionally measured floorplan measure as much as 50 to 200 ft bigger than the authorized strata plan. Whereas these measures search to eek out each portion of useable area, the outcomes can at no time be used to find out the true market gross worth.

If true market worth in a topic constructing at a stated ground stage is \$1100 per sq. foot then a distinction of fifty to 200 sq. ft may have a huge effect on the funding/perceived market worth.

Instance:

• Strata plan authorized sq. footage of 1575 sq. ft x \$1100 sqft = \$1,732,500
• Ground planner sq. footage of 1675 sq. ft x \$1100 sqft = \$1,842,500

An inventory agent that advertises the skilled measures sq. footage on the A number of Itemizing Service should disclose this in writing at time of the displaying/providing on the market.

When the client indicators their authorized paperwork at time of completion with their notary or lawyer, they’re signing off on the authorized strata plan and sq. footage; that is no time to have a shock or battle concerning the authorized sq. footage of their funding.

## Totally different Models of Measurement

Utilizing sq. ft is the commonest unit of measurement in American actual property. However it’s not your solely choice. For small tasks, you may need to work in sq. inches. For large tasks, like landscaping, sq. yards may make extra sense. And in worldwide actual property markets, sq. meters are the usual for dwelling measurements.

No matter your unit of measurement, the formulation is identical. Multiply the size occasions the width to calculate the world of sq. and rectangular surfaces. Simply be sure you’re utilizing the identical unit of measurement to your size and width. When you’re searching for sq. ft, measure each distances in ft; in case you’re searching for sq. meters, measure each distances in meters.

## Suppose once more

I’ve had clients cancel their functions to items of over 800-square ft, choosing the next 690-square-foot one bed room unit as a result of the format higher served their wants.

The direct entry to the dwelling quarters, sq. rooms, and lack of hallway means this house neatly makes use of essentially the most space for the obtainable sq. footage.

## Why Tenants, Owners, and Landlords Must Know Sq. Footage

There are a number of the reason why tenants, householders, and landlords ought to all know tips on how to calculate sq. ft:

• Realizing the sq. footage of a room might help you affirm in case your furnishings will match.
• Realizing the sq. footage of a selected floor might help you estimate renovation prices. When you’re changing a kitchen countertop, for instance, that you must calculate the sq. footage of the countertop so you may get correct quotes for the price of the job.
• When you understand how to calculate sq. ft, you’ll be able to be sure you order the correct amount of provides and supplies. If, for instance, you intend to color a wall that’s 12 ft lengthy by 10 ft tall, that you must discover the whole sq. footage so you understand how a lot paint to purchase.
• Maybe most significantly, figuring out the sq. footage of properties and residences helps you evaluate costs to seek out the most effective worth. Let’s say you’re deciding between two related residences: Condo A is \$1,500 monthly and Condo B is \$1,800 monthly. Which is the higher deal? Nicely, it depends upon the sq. footage. If Condo A is 500 sq. ft and Condo B is 1,000 sq. ft, you’re getting extra space to your cash with Condo B.

## Condo Area and How You Use It

When you’re solely utilizing your house as a spot to sleep, then a smaller micro or studio would in all probability go well with you higher than a big house. When you incessantly entertain and love internet hosting dinner events, you’ll need an house with a beneficiant eating area. In case your mother visits usually from out of city and also you want a spot for her to remain, then you definately may need to take into account a one or two-bedroom house fairly than a studio.  When you work from home, take into account what sort of area you’ll want to your workplace and the place you’ll need it to be positioned.

## What to go away out

An excellent rule of thumb to make sure you’re taking correct measurements is to exclude area you’ll be able to’t stroll on or dwell in. These kind of areas don’t depend as “gross dwelling space.”

“Somebody may suppose, ‘If I get the measurement of my first ground and I’ve a two-story home, I simply multiply that by two,’” Day says. Nonetheless, if that first ground features a two-story lobby, you’ll be able to’t depend the non-usable area.

Basements and garages, even when they’re completed, don’t typically depend towards whole sq. footage. Basements are usually excluded as a result of they’re constructed beneath grade, that means beneath floor stage. In case your state does permit basements to be included within the whole sq. footage of a house, although, you’ll doubtless want an ingress and egress, or a secure option to enter and exit the basement to the surface.

Completed attic areas — with some rules, together with ceiling heights — can depend towards the whole sq. footage of your property. If you’re planning to promote your property, work with an actual property agent to craft a list that precisely displays your property.

## Sq. footage is a crucial issue when renting

Measuring an house or home’s sq. footage helps decide its worth. Whereas figuring out this info might help you resolve if a property is well worth the lease being charged, keep in mind that calculating sq. footage is subjective.

Some landlords and actual property brokers could use ANSI tips and a few not. Sure states require sq. footage in each itemizing description whereas others don’t. That is why it is as much as you to determine it out your self or rent knowledgeable to do it for you.

# About phase margin of inverting amplifiers

Phase margin is usually defined as how far is the phase of the loop gain from -180: $$PM = \angle \text({loop \space gain}) +180^{\circ}$$

Let's suppose that we want to compute the maximum phase margin of a single-pole amplifier. For a non-inverting amplifier, this calculation is straightforward: PM(max) = -90 + 180 = 90. However, when it comes to inverting amplifiers, things can get tricky because of the initial 180 phase delay that already exists at low frequencies between the input and output.

For example, using the previous definition, one can compute PM(max) = (-180 - 90) + 180 = 90 or PM(max) = (+180 - 90) + 180 = 270. Thus, we get different answers depending on how we consider the initial phase delay (+180 or -180). However, intuition suggests that the correct value should be PM(max) = 90 because it is the amount of phase delay left until the input signal "inverts again".

What is right value of the phase margin in this case ?

• When the loop is closed around an amplifier, the feedback needs to be negative otherwise the closed loop is unstable. An inverting amplifier automatically gives negative feedback due to the inherent 180 phase shift, so when the output is fed back and added to the input, this is effectively a subtraction. If you go through the same process with a non-inverting amplifier you get positive feedback, hence instability.
– Chu
Jun 18, 2020 at 7:32
• The first calculation (-180 - 90) + 180 = 90 is incorrect; it is -90. -90 and 270 are the same phase.
– AJN
Jun 18, 2020 at 13:05

Quote:

Phase margin is usually defined as how far is the phase of the loop gain from -180: PM=∠(loop gain)+180∘

I would not agree to this definition. The LOOP GAIN is the gain of the complete loop when it is opened at a suitable point. Hence, it contains also the summing junction and the corresponding sign (negative or positive feedback).

Therefore, the definition does not make a difference between positive and negative feedback and the phase margin is simply the "distance" to the point of instability (oscillation condition):

When the magnitude of the loop gain is unity, the phase shift of the loop gain must not yet have reached the critical value of -360 deg (and the difference to 360 deg is the margin PM).

Comment: The problem with the quoted (simplified) definition is the following: There are many circuits with negative feedback where the minus sign is NOT introduced at the summing junction but anywhere within the loop. Some other circuits have negative and positive feedback pathes at the same time. In all these cases, it is necessary to use the 360deg criterion.

Given that an amplifier may be inverting or non-inverting at DC, you should take that into account when calculating phase margin. So, phase margin comes down to how much the phase angle has moved (i.e. |difference|) as frequency rises to the point where the |gain| becomes unity. Then you can use the formula: -

$$PM = \text{180}-|\angle_{\text{change from DC}}|$$

For an AC amplifier with no gain at DC it's more complicated because you then have to compute the frequency where the "normal" operating point is meant to be hence, you might consider this: -

$$PM = \text{180}-|\angle_{\text{change from normal operating point}}|$$

For the above we are interested in knowing if the circuit can produce an oscillating condition when an external feedback loop is applied around the amplifier.

